This class is meant to allow the use of lambdas for exception handling instead of writing try-catch-finally lines.

Example:
```java
// This will automatically print a stacktrace to console as a default method of handling of any class extending Exception
Handle.throwable(() -> {
    // Do something that might throw an error
});

// This will run the consumer to handle "any" Exception you may also filter them by checking the class or "escapedClass()"
Handle.throwable(() -> {
    // Do something that might throw an error
}, (ex) -> {
    // Handle the error here
});

// You may also handle a finally section, however it may be a bit confusion since you will need to use an AtomicReference
Handle.throwable(() -> {
    // Do something that might throw an error
}, (ex) -> {
    // Handle the error here
}, () -> {
    // Atomically do final actions like closing a reader
});
```

You can even use try-with-resources, for example you have some connection `myDatabase`
```java
Handle.withResource(myDatabase::getConnection, (connection) -> {
    connection.createStatement();
});

Handle.withResource(myDatabase::getConnection, (connection) -> {
    connection.createStatement();
}, (ex) -> {
    // Handle some error
});

Handle.withResource(myDatabase::getConnection, (connection) -> {
    connection.createStatement();
}, (ex) -> {
    // Handle some error
}, () -> {
    // Handle some finally
});
```
Your first parameter will  be the some method that retrieves a resource and it will be closed if an exception is thrown or when the try block finishes executing.

